import{_ as d,C as i,o as l,c as h,ag as o,E as a,j as t,a as r}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Agentic software engineering","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"agentic-engineering.md","filePath":"agentic-engineering.md"}'),c={name:"agentic-engineering.md"};function u(p,e,f,g,w,m){const s=i("CallGraph"),n=i("WalkThrough");return l(),h("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o('<h1 id="agentic-software-engineering" tabindex="-1">Agentic software engineering <a class="header-anchor" href="#agentic-software-engineering" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agentic software engineering&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Research software must be correct, and a wrong result that nobody notices is expensive. A coding agent writes code fast, but it also judges its own work too kindly, and a long test run fills its context. The harness therefore divides the work of a change among several agents. A plan states what done means before the work starts, one agent builds, another agent judges, and the long runs go to agents of their own.</p><h2 id="sub-agents" tabindex="-1">Sub-agents <a class="header-anchor" href="#sub-agents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sub-agents&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A session can start a sub-agent for one job. The sub-agent works in a context of its own and returns one short report, so the output of its job never fills the context of the session. The harness gives sub-agents three kinds of jobs:</p><ul><li><strong>A long job</strong>, such as a test suite: <a href="./components/agents#julia-test-runner"><code>julia-test-runner</code></a> runs it and returns pass or fail per testset, with the failures.</li><li><strong>A wide job</strong>, such as a change across many repositories: one sub-agent for each repository, and one verdict for each.</li><li><strong>A bounded diagnosis</strong>, such as a package that does not load: <a href="./components/agents#julia-load-doctor"><code>julia-load-doctor</code></a> returns the cause and the smallest fix.</li></ul><p>A sub-agent costs a new context, so a session starts one only where a fresh context or a smaller model does the work better. <a href="./components/agents#reader"><code>reader</code></a> and <a href="./components/agents#worker"><code>worker</code></a> load no <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so they start with a small context.</p><h2 id="from-a-plan-to-a-pull-request" tabindex="-1">From a plan to a pull request <a class="header-anchor" href="#from-a-plan-to-a-pull-request" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;From a plan to a pull request&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A change that needs more than a few edits goes through a plan:</p><ol><li><strong>Plan the parts.</strong> <a href="./components/skills#plan-parts"><code>plan-parts</code></a> splits the task into parts, and each part is one pull request. Each part gets a spec: its <em>Done when</em>, which states the acceptance criteria as checks; the edge cases decided in advance; the mutants that its tests must catch; and its files. A collision map shows which parts touch the same files, and so which parts can run at the same time. The plan is a task file.</li><li><strong>Choose the tier of each part.</strong> A <code>decision</code> part waits for you. A <code>build</code> part runs the full builder-critic loop of <a href="./components/skills#build-part"><code>build-part</code></a>. A <code>reviewed</code> part is small, with a fast gate, and runs <a href="./components/skills#build-reviewed"><code>build-reviewed</code></a>: one builder and one critic. A <code>direct</code> part is a mechanical change whose every check is a command, such as a rename across many repositories; the session or a workflow makes it, with no critic.</li><li><strong>Build.</strong> A builder writes the tests first, then the code, in a worktree of its own. For each guard, branch and comparison of its change, it writes a mutant: a copy of the code with one deliberate defect, which a good test must catch.</li><li><strong>Judge.</strong> A critic that has not seen the builder&#39;s report or reasoning judges the result against the <em>Done when</em>. It runs the checks itself, runs each named mutant, and probes the edges of each clause. A FAIL goes back to a new builder, which fixes the blocking defects.</li><li><strong>Open the pull request.</strong> After a PASS, a builder opens the pull request. In <code>build-part</code>, a question inside the loop goes to the <a href="./components/agents#advisor"><code>advisor</code></a>, and a loop that does not converge goes to the <a href="./components/agents#arbitrator"><code>arbitrator</code></a>, not to you.</li></ol><p>The critic is blind for a reason: a context that wrote the code does not judge it well. A fresh context, with no knowledge of the builder&#39;s reasons, finds more defects.</p><h2 id="one-worktree-for-each-part" tabindex="-1">One worktree for each part <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-worktree-for-each-part" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One worktree for each part&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each part is built in a worktree: a second checkout of the repository on its own branch, below <code>~/Research/.worktrees/</code>. The main checkout stays on <code>main</code>, and two parts can be built at the same time in two worktrees. Each new builder and critic of the same part works on the same worktree. <a href="./research-tree#temporary-work">The research tree</a> says where the worktrees are.</p><h2 id="pull-requests" tabindex="-1">Pull requests <a class="header-anchor" href="#pull-requests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pull requests&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Three agents work on a pull request of a Julia package:</p><ul><li><a href="./components/agents#julia-branch-verifier"><code>julia-branch-verifier</code></a> checks and fixes the branch before the pull request opens, on the diff only.</li><li><a href="./components/agents#julia-pr-reviewer"><code>julia-pr-reviewer</code></a> reviews the pull request blind, posts the review, and reads the CI jobs.</li><li><a href="./components/agents#julia-pr-shepherd"><code>julia-pr-shepherd</code></a> has the review done, fixes the findings and pushes the fix.</li></ul><p>No agent merges a pull request that its own session opened. The command <a href="./components/commands#merge-pr"><code>merge-pr</code></a> merges only a pull request that its session did not open or push to, once the required checks are green.</p><h2 id="models-and-efforts" tabindex="-1">Models and efforts <a class="header-anchor" href="#models-and-efforts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Models and efforts&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An agent names a <a href="./concepts#tier">tier</a>: <code>large</code>, <code>medium</code> or <code>small</code>. The model tables of the profile give the model of each tier for each frontend. A builder and a critic need a large model; a test run needs only a small one, so the large contexts do not wait on it. An agent can also name an <a href="./concepts#effort">effort</a>. The figures below show the model and the effort of each agent.</p><h2 id="who-spawns-whom" tabindex="-1">Who spawns whom <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-spawns-whom" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Who spawns whom&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An agent can start another agent. This page calls this a <em>spawn</em>: the first agent gives a task to a new agent, the new agent works in a context of its own, and it returns one report. Some skills spawn agents too. A skill runs in the session that loads it, so the session does the spawn.</p><p>Each figure below is a call graph: a box is an agent or a skill, and an arrow goes from the caller to the agent that it spawns. The text on an arrow says when or why the caller spawns the agent.</p><h3 id="how-to-read-a-figure" tabindex="-1">How to read a figure <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-to-read-a-figure" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How to read a figure&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each box shows three facts:</p><ul><li><strong>The name</strong>, in bold. A blue box is an agent of <code>agents/</code>. A yellow box is a skill of <code>skills/</code>.</li><li><strong>The model.</strong> The source names a model tier, <code>large</code>, <code>medium</code> or <code>small</code>, and the <code>[claude]</code> table of <code>examples/models.toml</code> gives the model of each tier: <code>opus</code>, <code>sonnet</code> and <code>haiku</code>. Your own <code>models.toml</code> changes these models (see <a href="./profile">profile.md</a>). A source with no <code>model:</code> runs on the model of its caller, and its box says &quot;the caller&#39;s model&quot;. A skill runs in the session that loads it, on the session&#39;s model, so a skill box says &quot;the session&#39;s model&quot;.</li><li><strong>The effort</strong>, from the source&#39;s <code>effort:</code>. A source with no <code>effort:</code> runs at the effort of the session, and its box says &quot;the session&#39;s effort&quot;. Where a caller sets a different effort for one spawn, the arrow says so: &quot;at high effort&quot;.</li></ul><p>The figures show Claude Code. OpenCode and oh-my-pi can run a council: copies of an agent on other models, each copy a seat. The figures do not draw the councils.</p><p>The arrows come from one file, <code>docs/figures/calls.toml</code>. It has one entry for each spawn, with the <code>file:line</code> of the instruction that spawns the agent. <code>harness test</code> checks each entry against the sources, and the docs build draws the figures from it. So a figure changes when an agent changes.</p><h3 id="the-builder-critic-loop-build-part" tabindex="-1">The builder-critic loop: <code>build-part</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-builder-critic-loop-build-part" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The builder-critic loop: `build-part`&quot;">​</a></h3>',27)),a(s,{root:"build-part",title:"The calls of build-part",desc:"The build-part skill spawns julia-builder or python-builder, julia-critic or python-critic in round 1 at high effort and in each verify round at its own effort, julia-test-runner, advisor and arbitrator. julia-builder spawns julia-branch-verifier and changelog-scribe."}),e[1]||(e[1]=o("<p>A part is one pull request of a plan. You start <code>build-part</code> in your session when a part is ready to build. The skill does not write code. It spawns a builder to build the part, and then a new critic to judge the result: <code>julia-builder</code> and <code>julia-critic</code> for a Julia part, <code>python-builder</code> and <code>python-critic</code> for a Python part, and <code>part-builder</code> and <code>part-critic</code> in a repository with neither a <code>Project.toml</code> nor a <code>pyproject.toml</code>. In the first critic round, two critics judge at high effort, and each one sees only the branch, never the builder&#39;s report. In each later verify round, which checks only the fix, the critic sees only the fix. Beside each critic, <code>julia-test-runner</code> runs the full test suite of a Julia part; for any other part the skill&#39;s session runs the repository&#39;s own suite in the background. A question inside the loop goes to the <code>advisor</code>, and a loop that does not converge goes to the <code>arbitrator</code>. Before the pull request of a Julia part, <code>julia-builder</code> spawns <code>julia-branch-verifier</code>, and it can spawn <code>changelog-scribe</code> for a fix of the CHANGELOG.</p><p>The work is split because a context that wrote the code does not judge it well. A fresh critic with no knowledge of the builder&#39;s reasons finds more defects. A long test run goes to an agent on a small model, so that the large contexts of the builder and the critic do not wait on it.</p>",2)),a(n,{name:"build-part"}),e[2]||(e[2]=o('<p><em>From a task to a pull request with build-part.</em> The <code>plan-parts</code> skill writes the parts table, the list of the parts of a plan, and <code>build-part</code> runs one part. In the first critic round, two critics judge the work at high effort beside a run of the full suite. After a FAIL, a new builder fixes the work, and one critic judges the fix. The second FAIL goes to the arbitrator. After a PASS, a new builder finishes the part and opens the pull request. The figure shows the default models of Claude Code. Your <code>models.toml</code> can add a council of critics on other models for OpenCode and oh-my-pi.</p><h3 id="a-smaller-loop-build-reviewed" tabindex="-1">A smaller loop: <code>build-reviewed</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-smaller-loop-build-reviewed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A smaller loop: `build-reviewed`&quot;">​</a></h3>',2)),a(s,{root:"build-reviewed",title:"The calls of build-reviewed",desc:"The build-reviewed skill spawns part-builder, part-critic and julia-test-runner. part-builder spawns julia-branch-verifier."}),e[3]||(e[3]=o("<p>A part of tier <code>reviewed</code> is small, with a fast gate. You start <code>build-reviewed</code> in a session on the large model. It spawns one <code>part-builder</code>, and a new one for each later round. When the builder is ready, the skill spawns one <code>part-critic</code>, which judges the work once, without seeing the builder&#39;s report. A run longer than five minutes goes to <code>julia-test-runner</code>. Before the pull request, the builder spawns <code>julia-branch-verifier</code>.</p><p>The split is smaller here: one builder, one critic. Your session reads the result before the finish, the last builder round, which opens the pull request. So your session is the second reviewer.</p>",2)),a(n,{name:"build-reviewed"}),e[4]||(e[4]=t("p",null,[t("em",null,"From a task to a pull request with build-reviewed."),r(" The skill spawns one "),t("code",null,"part-builder"),r(", and one "),t("code",null,"part-critic"),r(" judges its work. After a FAIL, a new builder fixes the findings, and your session checks the fix before it tells the builder to finish the part. With a PASS, your session reads the result, and then it tells the builder to finish the part.")],-1)),e[5]||(e[5]=t("h3",{id:"from-review-to-green-ci-julia-pr-shepherd",tabindex:"-1"},[r("From review to green CI: "),t("code",null,"julia-pr-shepherd"),r(),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#from-review-to-green-ci-julia-pr-shepherd","aria-label":'Permalink to "From review to green CI: `julia-pr-shepherd`"'},"​")],-1)),a(s,{root:"julia-pr-shepherd",title:"The calls of julia-pr-shepherd",desc:"The julia-pr-shepherd agent spawns julia-pr-reviewer, changelog-scribe and ci-triage."}),e[6]||(e[6]=o('<p>You spawn <code>julia-pr-shepherd</code> with a pull request of a Julia package. It does not review the pull request itself: it spawns <code>julia-pr-reviewer</code>, which posts the review. Then the shepherd fixes the findings. When the fix changes behaviour, it spawns <code>changelog-scribe</code> for the CHANGELOG entry. When CI is red in several jobs, it spawns <code>ci-triage</code> to separate the real failures from the known ones.</p><p>The review comes from a context that has not decided how to fix anything. So the findings are not shaped by the fix.</p><h3 id="every-spawn" tabindex="-1">Every spawn <a class="header-anchor" href="#every-spawn" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Every spawn&quot;">​</a></h3>',3)),a(s,{title:"Every spawn",desc:"Every edge of calls.toml: the build-part, build-reviewed, plan-parts and julia-structure skills, and the julia-builder, part-builder and julia-pr-shepherd agents, with each agent that they spawn."}),e[7]||(e[7]=o("<p>This figure shows every entry of <code>calls.toml</code>. It adds two skills: <code>plan-parts</code> sends each part&#39;s spec to a <code>reader</code> that measures its facts, and <code>julia-structure</code> sends a claim that nothing uses a name to <code>exhaustive-auditor</code>. An agent that is not in this figure is spawned only from your session. Only the agents with the tool <code>agent</code> can spawn another agent: <code>julia-builder</code>, <code>part-builder</code> and <code>julia-pr-shepherd</code>. Each spawn costs a new context, so an agent spawns another only where a fresh context or a smaller model does the work better.</p>",1))])}const T=d(c,[["render",u]]);export{k as __pageData,T as default};
